esp32s31: drain the recorder while it records, and stop it stealing DRM master

Two changes that together turn a capture bounded by RAM into a session bounded
by the SD card, and one bug that made the first attempt film nothing at all.

**The buffer was not a ring.** It filled and stopped - the comment said so, on
the reasoning that silently discarding the beginning is worse than a short
file. True when nothing drained it. It is now genuinely circular with a
consumer: seq and read count frames produced and consumed, frames are never
split across the wrap, and when there is no room the NEWEST frame is dropped
and counted. Nothing is silent: the sequence number is handed to userspace, so
a gap is visible to whatever is writing the file.

**Frames can be read while recording.** esp32s31_jpeg_rec_frame() used to
return -EBUSY unless the recording had been stopped, which capped any recording
at what fitted in RAM. It now pops the oldest frame whether or not the recorder
is running, and only reclaims the space after the copy to userspace has
succeeded, so a failed copy loses nothing.

**DRIVER_RENDER, because the recorder was taking DRM master.** The first
end-to-end attempt recorded 13 frames and then nothing for two and a half
minutes. The drainer starts from an init script at S02 and opened
/dev/dri/card0 - and the first process to open the primary node becomes DRM
master, so lvdesk at S40 got "SET_MASTER: Resource busy", then "SETCRTC:
Permission denied", and exited. The panel sat on a dead console; because
nothing committed, the recorder faithfully filmed a blank screen. The ioctls
were already DRM_RENDER_ALLOW, so a render node is all that was missing.

**The boot buffer is 3 MB, and is handed back.** A dense console frame is ~85 KB
at q60, so 1 MB held only 12 frames and 25 were dropped before the drainer
attached - five seconds of boot lost. The drainer now drains the backlog and
then restarts the recorder with the session settings, which frees the boot
buffer and switches to the desktop's rate and quality. Holding 3 MB on a 15 MB
machine matters; holding it only while the machine is otherwise empty does not.

Measured end to end: armed at 1.73 s, 560 frames over 170 s of boot, desktop
start and desktop use, 0 malformed frames, one sequence discontinuity - the
handover itself.

